{"answer":"For multimodal LLMs (especially diffusion-style dMLLMs), several decoding strategies target different failure modes by changing which masked/token positions get committed and when. Information-Guided Frontier Decoding (IGFD) improves decoding by ranking candidates using token confidence plus neighborhood uncertainty and a structural commitment risk term, aiming to commit reliable semantic anchors earlier and delay fragile structural tokens, without extra training or extra forward passes. [1] Context-Aware Cluster Decoding (CACD) similarly scores masked positions using a composite of softmax confidence and neighbor proximity, and it uses a block-free design to keep high-readiness semantic anchors globally accessible, also without training. [3]

In parallel diffusion multimodal decoding, Visual-Redundancy-Controlled Decoding (VRCD) addresses the step-level issue that confidence-based top-K selection can commit visually redundant tokens together; it uses a Visual Redundancy Index and prioritizes visually complementary positions via token-to-image attention to reduce redundancy and improve accuracy. [2] If the concern is hallucinations, Causal Decoding intervenes during generation to attenuate spurious dependencies that lead to false object tokens. [4] Learning Inference-time Modality Enhancement (LIME) uses relevance propagation to shift relevance toward perceptual tokens during decoding to reduce multimodal hallucinations. [9]

These differ mainly in what they optimize: commitment order/context propagation (IGFD/CACD), redundancy control in parallel position selection (VRCD), and faithfulness/hallucination mitigation via causal intervention or relevance shifting (Causal decoding/LIME). [1,2,3,4,9]","grounded":true}
